  "instructions": {
    "title": "HOW TO USE THIS TRACKING MATRIX",
    "steps": [
      {
        "step": "1. Page Tracker",
        "guidance": "Enter one row per page. Assign separate developers for UI, API, SP, Database and UI Binding. Update each component status; Progress % calculates automatically."
      },
      {
        "step": "2. Bug Tracker",
        "guidance": "Log every bug. Original Developer is the person who created the affected component. Fix Developer is the person who resolves the bug."
      },
      {
        "step": "3. Parent / Child Bugs",
        "guidance": "For a parent bug, leave Parent Bug ID blank and select Parent. For an internal child bug, enter the Parent Bug ID, select Child and set Internal Bug? to Yes."
      },
      {
        "step": "4. Reopened Bugs",
        "guidance": "Set Reopened? to Yes and increase No. of Reopens whenever a fix fails retesting."
      },
      {
        "step": "5. Developer Scorecard",
        "guidance": "Automatically calculates component workload, original defect quality, fix quality and final developer score."
      },
      {
        "step": "6. QA Scorecard",
        "guidance": "Automatically calculates pages tested, bugs raised, valid bugs, rejected/duplicate bugs and QA accuracy. Missed Bugs remains a controlled manual input."
      },
      {
        "step": "7. Dashboard",
        "guidance": "All KPIs and charts are formula-driven. Do not type over calculated cells."
      },
      {
        "step": "8. Gantt",
        "guidance": "Basic module-wise Gantt is generated from Planned Start, Planned End, Actual End and page Progress %."
      },
      {
        "step": "9. Lists",
        "guidance": "Update developer and QA names in Lists. Use exactly the same names throughout the workbook."
      }
    ],
